# Cube Voyager control-statement scripts (.s/.block) are this project's
# actual subject matter, wherever they're committed — today's fixture corpus
# (valid/, broken/, golden/, encoding_fallback/, and their nested
# real_corpus/ subtrees under crates/voyager-core/tests/fixtures/), and any
# future fixture directory a later phase adds (e.g. 003-lsp-server test
# fixtures, wherever in the tree they land). format()/format_bytes()
# (002-cli-check-format, FR-012) deliberately preserves each line's original
# line-ending style rather than normalizing it, so letting Git's
# core.autocrlf normalize these on checkout produces byte-level corruption
# that looks exactly like real formatter drift in every golden-file/
# idempotency comparison. An extension-based rule closes this permanently,
# rather than a directory-based carve-out that has to be remembered and
# re-added every time a new fixture directory appears — which is exactly how
# this bug reached main once already (see git history: core.autocrlf=true
# silently rewrote tests/fixtures/golden/ to CRLF on the
# 002-cli-check-format -> main fast-forward merge, because the rule at the
# time was scoped to specific subdirectories that didn't include it).
*.s -text
*.block -text

# mdBook's committed build output (022-docs-site, research.md §2 — served
# directly by GitHub Pages from docs/, not through a GitHub Actions deploy
# step). Generated HTML/CSS/JS, never hand-edited -- core.autocrlf rewriting
# it on checkout makes every rebuild show a spurious full-file diff against
# whatever a contributor's local line-ending setting produces, even when
# scripts/build-docs.ps1 regenerated byte-identical content, which defeats
# the whole point of the freshness check (research.md §6) being a precise
# signal. Same fix, same rationale, as the *.s/*.block rule above -- applied
# here before this became a second instance of that exact bug class rather
# than after.
docs/** -text

# Vendored reference data: fetched verbatim from an external authoritative
# source (or hand-curated as primary research evidence) and worth protecting
# by content, not just current use — so no contributor's local autocrlf
# setting can silently make their checkout diverge byte-for-byte from
# everyone else's, even for files nothing currently byte-compares against.
# The SARIF schema is parsed, not diffed, today; it's still the authoritative
# copy this project checks conformance against, so its provenance matters.
crates/drut-cli/tests/schemas/*.json -text
specs/**/*.csv -text
